The '''Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal''' ({{Langx|ca|Estació Terminal Central de Sant Cristòfor}}) is the primary railway station of [[Sant Cristòfor]], [[Balboa]], and the principal rail hub of the country. Located in [[Original (ciutat)|Original]], the historic core of the city, the terminal is the terminus for all [[CristoCarril]] commuter rail services operating from the city, six long-distance rail lines connecting to every province, and two high-speed rail lines to [[Portnou]] and the [[International City of Areza|Areza]]. The station also serves as a major interchange for the [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor]], with three metro lines terminating at the station on an upper underground level and three further lines passing through on a deeper underground north–south alignment.

The terminal was constructed between 1906 and 1911 during the [[Zong Balboa]] era, replacing the original 1868–1872 Isthmus Railway terminus on the same site. Commissioned by Governor [[Tan Tek-chiu]] and opened under Governor [[Lîm Bûn-cheng]] on 16 February 1911, the building is noted for its ornate architectural fusion of Volisanian colonial and Zong imperial styles. The terminal was damaged during the [[Great Adonian War]] siege of Sant Cristòfor and was restored in 1953.

== History ==

=== Original station (1868–1872) ===

The first railway station on the site was constructed between 1868 and 1872 as the western terminus of the [[Isthmus Railway]], the Volisanian-owned railway line that crossed the Isthmus of Balboa from [[Sant Cristòfor]] on the Shendan coast to [[Sant Llàtzer]] on the Aethiopean coast. The station's construction was mired in the [[Escàndol Ferroviari]] of 1866, a political scandal in which the Isthmus Railway Company and Zong shipping interests were found to have bribed the Balboan National Party to secure favourable railway concessions. The Isthmus Railway Company, which owned the line, paid only land rent to the Balboan government and operated the railway as a private concern with minimal public oversight.

The original station was a modest Volisanian colonial structure, built to serve the limited traffic of the isthmus crossing. The station's political significance, however, exceeded its commercial role: it was on the steps of this terminal that Mayor [[Joan Ballester i Forteza]] was assassinated in 1886, an event that became a focal point of Balboan political discontent with the Volisanian railway concession and contributed to the broader tensions that culminated in the [[Second War of the Isthmus]].

