=== Zong Balboa reconstruction (1906–1911) ===

Following the [[Treaty of Sant Llàtzer]] (1899), which transferred sovereignty over Sant Cristòfor and the canal zone to the [[Kingdom of Zong]], the existing railway infrastructure came under Zong administration. Governor [[Tan Tek-chiu]] (1899–1910) commissioned the reconstruction of the terminal on the same site in 1906, as part of a broader programme of infrastructure investment that accompanied the establishment of Zong governance. The original 1868–1872 station was demolished and replaced with a substantially larger structure designed to serve the expanded rail network that the Zong administration envisioned for the territory.

Construction proceeded over five years and was completed in early 1911. The terminal opened on 16 February 1911 under Tan's successor, Governor [[Lîm Bûn-cheng]] (1910–1925), who had assumed office the previous year. The opening ceremony was attended by Zong colonial officials, local dignitaries, and representatives of the commercial community, and was reported in both the Volisanian- and Zong-language presses of the territory. The reconstructed terminal provided 30 at-grade tracks served by 20 platforms — a dramatic expansion from the original station — and incorporated underground tracks for the first two lines of the [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor]], which were built concurrently beneath the terminal. The underground station was the first underground railway infrastructure in Sant Cristòfor and was constructed using cut-and-cover methods beneath the terminal's at-grade platforms.

=== Great Adonian War and restoration (1935–1953) ===

The terminal suffered significant damage during the [[Great Adonian War]], which opened in 1935 with the [[Balboa Campaign]] — a joint Volisanian and Balboan assault on Sant Cristòfor. The city, defended by its walls and sustained by Zong naval supply lines, endured a siege that lasted for the duration of the war from 1935 to 1947. The terminal's train shed and roof structures were damaged by sustained aerial bombing, and the station's operations were disrupted for much of the siege, though the underground metro platforms provided shelter for civilians during air raids.

Following the end of the war in 1947 and the establishment of the [[Adonian Community]] mandate over Sant Cristòfor, the terminal was restored in 1953 under the administration of Mayor [[Elena Vargas i Aldana]]. The restoration preserved the terminal's original architectural character, including the distinctive fusion of Volisanian colonial and Zong imperial elements, while repairing structural damage and modernising the station's mechanical systems.

=== Post-reunification expansion ===

The post-reunification period, following the return of Sant Cristòfor to Balboa in 1960, saw significant expansion of the metro infrastructure beneath the terminal. The planned Metro Line 3, a north–south connector whose construction had been halted by the war, was completed in 1964 with underground through tracks and platforms beneath the terminal. Metro Line 4 was added in 1968, terminating at the station alongside the existing Lines 1 and 2. Line 5, opened in 1971, was routed through the underground north–south tracks. Line 7 was added in the 1990s as part of the expansion of the metro network to the outer ciutats, also utilising the through tracks.

The high-speed rail network was connected to the terminal in the late 2000s. The [[Sant Cristòfor–Areza HSR line]] opened in 2008, followed by the [[Sant Cristòfor–Portnou HSR line]] in 2009. Both lines share a common trunk between the Central Terminal and [[Varakí TDV (CristoCarril)|Varakí TDV]], diverging south of Varakí to their respective destinations.

== Architecture ==

The Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal is an architectural fusion of Volisanian colonial and Zong imperial styles, reflecting the political and cultural circumstances of its construction during the early Zong Balboa era. The building was designed to serve both as a functional rail terminus and as a statement of the new administration's ambition and cultural sophistication.

=== Exterior ===

The terminal's principal facade faces south onto the main concourse approach. The facade is organised as a series of Volisanian colonial arcades at ground level, with ornate ironwork detailing typical of the colonial architectural tradition. Above the arcades, the upper levels incorporate Zong imperial elements: upturned eaves at the roofline, decorative tilework panels set into the wall surfaces, and a central courtyard concept expressed through an open loggia at the upper level. The combination of the two traditions produces a facade that is neither wholly Volisanian nor wholly Zong, but a synthesis specific to the Zong Balboa territory.

<!-- Image prompt: Architectural detail shot of a railway terminal facade, ground-level Volisanian colonial arches with ornate ironwork, upper level with Zong imperial decorative tilework panels in blue and green geometric patterns, upturned eaves at roofline, warm tropical light, close-up architectural photography -->
[[File:Sant Cristofor Central Terminal facade.png|thumb|250px|Detail of the south facade, showing Volisanian colonial arcades at ground level and Zong imperial tilework and upturned eaves above]]

The roofline is marked by a series of upturned eaves along the central block, a direct reference to Zong imperial architecture, while the flanking wings retain the lower, horizontal rooflines characteristic of Volisanian colonial railway buildings. The decorative tilework, produced by artisans brought from the Zong heartland, incorporates geometric and floral motifs that contrast with the classical detailing of the colonial arcades below.

=== Concourse ===

The main concourse occupies the headhouse at the southern end of the terminal. The space is a large, rectangular hall with a high ceiling, lit by clerestory windows and a series of skylights set into the roof. The concourse walls feature Volisanian colonial arcading at ground level, with the upper walls surfaced in decorative tilework in the Zong tradition. The floor is laid with patterned tiles that combine geometric motifs from both traditions. The concourse houses ticketing halls, waiting areas, and retail spaces, and provides access to the at-grade platforms through a series of wide archways.

