== Layout ==

The Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal is a multi-level station complex, with at-grade rail platforms and underground metro infrastructure. The station is oriented roughly north–south for the main rail alignment, with the headhouse and concourse at the southern end and the platforms extending northward.

=== At-grade platforms ===

The terminal has 30 at-grade tracks served by 20 platforms, all at ground level, serving rail services exclusively. The platforms comprise a mix of side platforms and island platforms arranged in a series of parallel tracks extending northward from the headhouse, covered by the train shed. The southern end of each platform terminates at the concourse, which provides the primary passenger access to the platforms. The 30 tracks serve [[CristoCarril]] Lines B, C, D, and E (and additional commuter services), six long-distance rail lines, and two high-speed rail lines. The platform allocation is organised by service type, with commuter rail platforms grouped to facilitate rapid passenger interchange, long-distance platforms located toward the centre of the shed, and high-speed rail platforms at the southern end nearest the concourse.

=== Underground metro levels ===

Beneath the at-grade platforms, the terminal incorporates underground metro infrastructure on two levels. The station box is a complex layered structure, with the upper level situated at shallow depth beneath the terminal and the deeper level constructed below.

The upper underground level houses four terminating platforms for [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro]] Lines 1, 2, and 4, which run in a west–east alignment and terminate at the station. Lines 1 and 2 occupy tracks 5 and 6, with Line 4 utilising the remaining two tracks. This level was constructed using cut-and-cover methods during the Shining Star era, when Lines 1 and 2 were the first metro lines built in Sant Cristòfor. Line 4 was added to the upper level in 1968.

The deeper underground level houses four through platforms with passing tracks for Metro Lines 3, 5, and 7, which run in a north–south alignment through the station. The through platforms are arranged as two island platforms, each serving two tracks, with the passing tracks located on the outer sides of each island. This configuration allows all-stop services to serve the station while express services pass through on the passing tracks without platform access. The deeper level was constructed using bored tunnels at greater depth beneath the upper level. Line 3 was the first line on this level, completed in 1964, followed by Line 5 in 1971 and Line 7 in the 1990s.

<!-- Image prompt: Underground metro station platform, deep level, two island platforms with through tracks and outer passing tracks, tiled walls with geometric patterns, fluorescent lighting, modern metro signage, train at platform, tunnel bore visible, subterranean transit photography -->
[[File:Sant Cristofor Central Terminal underground platform.png|thumb|250px|The deeper underground metro level, showing the island platform arrangement with passing tracks]]

The layered configuration of the station box — with the west–east terminating tracks above the north–south through tracks — requires complex structural engineering to maintain separation between the two levels. The Line 1 extension to Barcelona project, under construction since 2026, requires modifications to the station box to connect the existing Line 1 and 2 tracks to a new underwater tunnel, threading through the layered infrastructure without disrupting the deeper north–south lines.

The underground station is integrated with the [[Tarifa Integrada]] fare system, permitting free transfers between metro lines and to CristoCarril and other transport services within a 90-minute window.

