Lines 1 and 2 were the first metro lines constructed in Sant Cristòfor, built during the Shining Star era on the upper underground level of the station, terminating at the terminal and running eastward to mainland neighbourhoods. Line 3, a north–south connector planned before the Great Adonian War, was completed in 1964 on the deeper underground level with through tracks and passing tracks beneath the upper level. Line 4 was added to the upper terminating level in 1968. Line 5 was routed through the deeper north–south tracks in 1971. Line 7 was added to the deeper level in the 1990s as part of the expansion to the outer ciutats. The three through lines (3, 5, 7) utilise the deeper underground north–south tracks with passing tracks that allow express services to bypass the station.

== Role in the network ==

The Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal is the principal rail hub of Balboa and the principal terminus for all rail services in the country. The terminal's 20 at-grade rail platforms (serving 30 tracks), 4 underground terminating metro platforms, and 4 underground through metro platforms make it the largest station in Balboa by platform count, and its position in the centre of Original — the historic core of Sant Cristòfor — places it at the convergence of the city's transport network.

The terminal's role as a multi-modal interchange is central to its function. The integration of commuter rail, long-distance rail, high-speed rail, and metro services within a single station complex allows passengers to transfer between modes without leaving the station, and the [[Tarifa Integrada]] fare system facilitates free transfers between services within a 90-minute window. The [[Ferries de la Badia de Sant Cristòfor|Sant Cristòfor Main ferry terminal]] is located across the plaça in front of the station, providing connections to the [[Barcelona (Sant Cristòfor)|Barcelona]] peninsula and the islands of the [[Badia de Sant Cristòfor]] via eight ferry routes. The terminal also serves as a major hub for the [[Sant Cristòfor bus network|bus network]], with multiple bus stops and a bus interchange located on the streets surrounding the station.

The terminal's position at the head of the Estret corridor, with all commuter and long-distance services radiating southward from the station, reflects the historical development of Balboa's rail network from the isthmus crossing. The high-speed rail lines, added in 2008 and 2009, extend this pattern into the modern era, with the common trunk to Varakí TDV serving as the spine of the national high-speed network.

